model {
  extend basalt.toolsGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "A native grant without models asks the pool nothing: carry 0681's per-check pool device check and its pool-access refusal apply only to grants with models. A tools-only grant's access checks, token issuance and refreshes, and tool calls are decided by the grant, its consent and token rows and live membership in its tools organization, so they keep working while the pool's control plane fails. A revoked tools-only grant is refused at once by the tools gateway's per-call reads of its tool calls, and by a remembered access answer within carry 0681's 3-second bound. Its access answer's display carries the account and the grant's pool id with an empty name and no pool choices. A tools-only approval never calls the pool's register: the host opens the pool's signed setup intent itself, requires the approving person to be its owner, checks live membership in its organization, and records the policy with the intent's pool and a computer id of 32 hex digits from SHA-256 of the grant id, which can't equal the pool's own UUIDs. The intent still comes from the pool's Connect context, and no longer than five minutes before. The pool directory's organization record is no longer compared with the intent's: live membership in the intent's organization bounds the device to what the person can already reach. The host then reports the device to the pool's native control as learn, in the background, bounded at 3 seconds, and the native devices endpoint lists the person's tools-only grants, live and revoked, at most 100 of each, for the pool's Connect list to add a device it missed and mark revoked one revoked while it was down. Revoking a tools-only grant tells the pool, marked as tools-only so a pool that never heard of it records it revoked, but succeeds whatever it answers. Revoking one's own native grant again answers revoked. A grant with models still registers at the pool, asks it on every check, refresh and tool call, and revokes its computer there first; the pool's outage refuses it as unavailable. A refused pool check answers with the reasoned refusal (revoked, or unavailable when the pool answers 503), and a revoked token, client or grant is refused before this check."
    }
  }
}
